# Responsible AI – Administrative Assistant (HR & Engineering Coordination)

This section applies the six responsible-use concepts from Day 3 to real tasks in my role.

---

## Accuracy
I ensure that any AI-generated summary or draft matches the actual HR request, engineering update, or project card details. I compare the output with the original source before using it.

## Accountability
I take responsibility for reviewing AI-generated content before sending it to HR, engineers, or project managers. My name is on the final communication, so I verify everything.

## Confidentiality
I avoid including restricted project details, internal engineering information, or sensitive HR data in any AI prompt. Confidential information is never shared with unapproved tools.

## Privacy
I do not enter employee names, ID numbers, phone numbers, or any personal identifiers into AI tools. When needed, I anonymize the information.

## Bias
I check that summaries or recommendations do not favor one employee, team, or department unfairly. I ensure the output reflects the full context, not a one‑sided view.

## Human Oversight
I always review and approve AI-generated content before sending it. Final decisions—especially HR or project-related—are made by humans, not AI.

---

## Information Classification (Green / Amber / Red)

| Information Item | Classification | Reason |
|------------------|----------------|--------|
| General HR coordination tasks | Green | Non-sensitive, general administrative information. |
| Engineering meeting summary (without names) | Amber | Internal operational details; requires judgment before sharing. |
| Restricted project access card request details | Red | Contains sensitive project information and must not be shared with AI. |

